# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 3 ways: locke-labour.logic.r3 has "labour_his ∧ mixed ∧ enough_left → no_other_right" and "removes ∧ no_other_right → excludes"; locke-labour.logic.r3.2 has "labour_his ∧ mixed ∧ enough_left → no_other_right" and "no_other_right → excludes"; they differ when only "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his", "he hath mixed his labour with the thing and joined to it something that is his own", "there is enough, and as good, left in common for others" and "no man but he can have a right to what his labour is once joined to" are true, which the first allows; locke-labour.logic.r3.3 has "labour_his ∧ mixed ∧ enough_left → no_other_right"
locke-labour.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r3.2, to: Suppose "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his", "he hath mixed his labour with the thing and joined to it something that is his own", "there is enough, and as good, left in common for others" and "no man but he can have a right to what his labour is once joined to" are true and "he removes a thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in" and "the thing hath by his labour something annexed to it that excludes the common right of other men" are false. Is S4, "It being by him removed from the common state nature hath placed it in, it hath by this labour something annexed to it, that excludes the common right of other men: for this labour being the unquestionable property of the labourer, no man but he can have a right to what that is once joined to, at least where there is enough, and as good, left in common for others.", true then?
locke-labour.logic.r3: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r3 and locke-labour.logic.r3.2 have "excludes → property"; locke-labour.logic.r3.3 has "no_other_right → excludes" and "excludes → property"; they differ when only "he thereby makes the thing his property" and "no man but he can have a right to what his labour is once joined to" are true, which the first allows
locke-labour.logic.r3: argue/readings: U1: locke-labour.logic.r3.2 and locke-labour.logic.r3.3 take for granted "if no man but he can have a right to what his labour is once joined to, then the thing hath by his labour something annexed to it that excludes the common right of other men"; locke-labour.logic.r3 does not
locke-labour.logic.r3: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r3.2 and locke-labour.logic.r3.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if no man but he can have a right to what his labour is once joined to, then the thing hath by his labour something annexed to it that excludes the common right of other men"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
